<section class="section" id="discovery">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="kicker">Step 3: point it at the unknown</p>
    <h2>25 real, unconfirmed TESS candidates</h2>
    <p>
      Same pipeline, real TESS Objects of Interest still flagged as planet candidates by the archive, nobody
      has fully verified these yet. For each one: download the light curve, detrend, run this pipeline's own
      BLS search bounded around the catalog's reported period, an independent check rather than just
      trusting the catalog value, then physically characterize whatever depth is used.
    </p>

    <div class="stat-row">
      <div class="stat">
        <span class="stat-num">25<span class="stat-den">/25</span></span>
        <span class="stat-label">candidates characterized</span>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <span class="stat-num">16<span class="stat-den">/25</span></span>
        <span class="stat-label">independently re-detected by this pipeline's own BLS, within 1%</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      Every candidate in this batch (all under 15-day orbital periods) comes back too hot for its star's
      habitable zone, physically expected: short orbital periods mean small semi-major axes. Full table
      with per-candidate radius, orbital distance, and equilibrium temperature (each with a Monte Carlo
      uncertainty range, not a single precise-looking number):
      <a href="https://github.com/AmjadAAYD/exoplanet-transit-detection/blob/main/docs/discovery_table.csv">discovery_table.csv</a>.
    </p>

    <p class="caveat">
      <strong>None of these are confirmed planets.</strong> This pipeline flags transit-like signals, it
      does not confirm them. Real confirmation needs follow-up spectroscopy or radial-velocity data outside
      the scope of this project, the same first step real discovery pipelines take, not a discovery in
      itself.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

